{"text":"The graph is invalid because `support` is not an allowed check field, and the requirement text is corrupted rather than preserving the exact instruction. Keep R1’s existing ID, exact source quote, and instruction text unchanged. Remove the unsupported `support` property from checks; represent evidence gathering only through the schema’s `role` and `dependencies` fields.

The source image clearly shows one blue mug to the left of the headphones. In the edited image, the headphones are smaller and shifted, and there are multiple mug-like objects, including a blue blurred shape and two pale mugs. The graph should therefore distinguish the original mug from newly introduced or duplicated mugs and assess whether that source object itself was moved to the headphones’ right. Do not treat the mere presence of a mug on the right as fulfillment, and do not infer successful correspondence where the edit makes identity uncertain.

Retain one applicable outcome covering R1 and exactly one requested check for it. The requested check should distinguish complete fulfillment (the source mug is identifiable and is to the right of the headphones), partial progress (the source mug is identifiable and has moved toward the right but is not yet to the right), no progress (the source mug remains in its original relative position, or only a separate mug was added), and genuinely insufficient evidence (identity or movement cannot be assessed). If a support check is useful, make it a separate support-role check in the same outcome, remove the invalid field, and list its ID as a dependency of the requested check; ensure every support is used, dependencies are acyclic, IDs are unique, and the total check count stays within four. Do not force a label or mark an unsuccessful edit inapplicable."}
